const quickAnswer = `The best TVs for bright rooms are high-nit QLED and Mini-LED models that can sustain 1,000–2,500 nits of peak brightness with an anti-reflective screen coating. Top performers: Samsung QN90D Neo QLED (1,500+ nits, excellent anti-glare), LG QNED90 (1,200 nits, wide viewing angles), Sony Bravia 9 XR Mini LED (2,000+ nits, best color accuracy), and Hisense U8 series (value option, 1,500+ nits). Avoid OLED for extremely bright rooms — OLED is phenomenal for dark rooms but tops out at 800–1,000 nits on most models and shows more glare than Mini-LED panels. The two numbers that matter most: peak brightness (nits) and anti-reflective coating type. A 2,000 nit TV with a standard glossy panel can look worse than a 1,200 nit TV with a proper anti-reflective matte coating in direct sunlight. For outdoor-facing rooms in San Antonio where afternoon sun hits the screen directly, you need both: high nits and a matte/anti-glare finish.`;

const fullAnswer = `<h2>Why Bright Rooms Demand Different Specs</h2>
<p>TV manufacturers optimize OLED panels for dark rooms where contrast (black levels) matters most. Bright room TVs need the opposite: enough sustained brightness to overcome ambient light, plus a screen coating that diffuses reflections rather than creating a mirror effect.</p>
<p>The key specification is <strong>sustained brightness</strong> (also called ABL — Automatic Brightness Limiter). A TV might advertise "2,000 nits peak" on a 10% window highlight, but sustain only 400 nits at full-screen. In a bright room, you are watching mostly full-screen content, so the sustained brightness matters far more than peak.</p>

<h2>Best TVs for Bright Rooms — 2024-2025</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>TV</th><th>Peak Nits</th><th>Sustained Nits</th><th>Anti-Glare</th><th>Best For</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Samsung QN90D Neo QLED</td><td>1,600</td><td>900+</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Best all-around bright room pick</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Sony Bravia 9 XR</td><td>2,000+</td><td>1,000+</td><td>Very Good</td><td>Best color accuracy in bright rooms</td></tr>
  <tr><td>LG QNED90</td><td>1,200</td><td>700+</td><td>Good</td><td>Wide viewing angles, open floor plans</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Hisense U8N</td><td>1,500</td><td>800+</td><td>Good</td><td>Best value bright room TV</td></tr>
  <tr><td>TCL QM8</td><td>1,800</td><td>900+</td><td>Good</td><td>Budget option with strong brightness</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Screen Coating: The Overlooked Factor</h2>
<p>A screen coating is not a gimmick — it is the difference between a TV that looks good in a bright room and one that turns into a mirror. Two main types:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Anti-reflective (matte) coating:</strong> Diffuses reflected light. The reflection is still there but spread across the surface rather than concentrated. Looks like a frosted matte image instead of a sharp mirror. Samsung Neo QLED panels use this approach.</li>
  <li><strong>Anti-glare (glossy with filter):</strong> High gloss with a polarized layer that reduces specular reflection. Better perceived black depth than matte but creates sharp dark reflections in some light angles. OLED models typically use this approach.</li>
</ul>
<p>For rooms with windows facing the screen or strong overhead lighting, <strong>matte anti-reflective coatings outperform glossy + polarizer</strong> in direct reflection scenarios. Samsung's Neo QLED series leads here.</p>

<h2>OLED vs QLED in Bright Rooms</h2>
<p><strong>In a dark or controlled-light room:</strong> OLED wins — infinite contrast, perfect blacks, faster response, thinner panel.<br>
<strong>In a bright room:</strong> QLED/Mini-LED wins — more sustained brightness, better anti-glare options, more affordable at larger sizes.</p>
<p>Samsung's QD-OLED panels (used in Sony A95L, Samsung S90D) are a middle ground: OLED contrast plus higher brightness than traditional WOLED, with a glossy screen that still shows some glare. Better than standard OLED in bright rooms, not as good as Neo QLED.</p>

<h2>Viewing Angle Considerations</h2>
<p>If viewers watch from multiple angles — a side couch, a kitchen island, a bar area — choose a TV with wide viewing angle technology. Samsung's Neo QLED models using VA panels can show color shift past 40° off-axis. LG's IPS-based QNED and NanoCell panels hold color at 60°+. Sony's Bravia 9 uses a VA panel with their X-Wide Angle layer for improved off-axis performance.</p>`;

const proTip = `The most important room diagnostic for bright TV placement: stand where the TV will go and look at the wall. Note the time of day when natural light is harshest. South and west-facing walls in San Antonio homes see the worst afternoon light from approximately 1–5 PM. If the TV is wall-mounted on a south or west wall with windows opposite it, you need either 1,500+ nit brightness or blackout shades, or ideally both. No TV can fully overcome a window directly behind the viewer flooding light at the screen.`;

